course: religion-ethics
lesson: 17
title: The Sermon on the Mount
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In Matthew's Gospel, chapters 5 to 7, Jesus sits on a hillside and teaches a crowd what life in God's kingdom looks like. Christians call it the Sermon on the Mount.
explain: It begins with the Beatitudes, blessings for people the world often overlooks: the poor in spirit, those who mourn, the meek, the merciful, the pure in heart and the peacemakers.
explain: Then come hard sayings: love your enemies, turn the other cheek, do to others what you would have them do to you, do not judge, do not worry, store up treasure in heaven.
explain: Jesus says his followers are salt and light: they should make the world better and let their good deeds be seen, not for their own praise but so that people give glory to God.
explain: For a child today it means: stop a fight instead of joining it, wish good even for someone who was mean, check your own faults before pointing at others, and care more about people than about stuff.
fact: The Sermon on the Mount is Jesus' great teaching on a hillside, found in Matthew chapters 5 to 7.
fact: The Beatitudes are blessings: «Blessed are the poor in spirit, those who mourn, the meek, the merciful, the pure in heart, the peacemakers».
fact: Jesus said, «Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God».
fact: Jesus called his followers «the salt of the earth» and «the light of the world».
fact: Jesus said, «Love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you».
fact: Jesus said that if someone strikes you on one cheek, you should offer the other instead of taking revenge.
fact: How far «turn the other cheek» applies, for example to self-defence or war, is disputed among Christians.
fact: Jesus' Golden Rule is «Do to others what you would have them do to you» (Matthew 7:12).
fact: Many other faiths, including Judaism and Islam, teach a rule very like the Golden Rule.
fact: Jesus told people not to worry about tomorrow, because God feeds the birds and clothes the lilies of the field.
fact: Jesus said, «Do not store up treasure on earth, where moth and rust destroy», and, «Where your treasure is, there your heart will be also».
fact: Jesus said to take the plank out of your own eye before taking the speck out of your brother's eye.
fact: Christians believe loving your enemy means wanting good for them; it does not mean letting them keep hurting you.
exercise: Where in the Bible is the Sermon on the Mount found? | Matthew chapters 5 to 7 | Genesis chapters 1 to 3 | Acts chapter 2 | Psalm 23
exercise: What are the Beatitudes? | Blessings for people the world often overlooks | Rules about food and washing | A list of the twelve apostles and the towns they came from | Songs sung at the Temple
exercise: Which of these is one of the Beatitudes? | Blessed are the merciful | Blessed are the richest | Blessed are the strongest | Blessed are the cleverest
exercise: What did Jesus say peacemakers will be called? | Children of God | Kings of the earth | Friends of Moses | Masters of the law
exercise: What did Jesus call his followers? | The salt of the earth and the light of the world | The bread of life and the true vine, and the good shepherd | The rock and the key | The sheep and the goats
exercise: Why should people let their good deeds be seen, according to Jesus? | So that others give glory to God | So that they get praised by everyone | So that they win prizes | So that others feel ashamed
exercise: What did Jesus say about enemies? | Love them and pray for them | Avoid them for ever and ever | Pay them back fairly for what they did | Pretend they are not there at all
exercise: Does loving your enemy mean letting them keep hurting you? | No, it means wanting good for them | Yes, you must never get help | Yes, you must do whatever they say | No, it means you may hurt them first
exercise: What does «turn the other cheek» teach? | Do not take revenge | Always hit back twice as hard | Look away when someone is hurt | Never speak to someone again
exercise: Do all Christians agree on how far «turn the other cheek» applies to war and self-defence? | No, it is disputed | Yes, all agree war is always right | Yes, all agree it applies to nothing | No, because Jesus never said it
exercise: What is Jesus' Golden Rule? | Do to others what you would have them do to you | Do to others what they did to you | Do to others only what your parents allow | Do to others what is easiest for you
exercise: Why did Jesus point to the birds and the lilies? | To show God cares for them, so people need not worry | To teach the crowd how to grow a better garden at home | To warn that birds eat the crops | To show that nature is dangerous
exercise: Where did Jesus say to store up treasure? | In heaven | In a strong box | In the Temple | Under the ground
exercise: What did Jesus mean by «where your treasure is, there your heart will be also»? | What we value shows where our heart is | Rich people have healthier hearts | Treasure should be hidden near home | You should keep money close to your chest
exercise: What should you take out of your own eye before helping your brother? | The plank | The speck | The tear | The dust
exercise: What does the plank and the speck teach? | Look at your own faults before criticising others | Always tell people about their faults straight away so they learn | Woodwork is dangerous for the eyes | Small faults are worse than big ones
test: My friend says the Beatitudes are blessings for the rich and powerful. What do you put right? | They bless people the world overlooks, like the meek and those who mourn | They bless kings and rulers who win wars | They bless the people who keep the most rules | They bless only the twelve disciples
test: «Two lads are about to fight behind the bike shed and everyone's chanting.» What would a peacemaker do? | Try to calm it down and get an adult if needed | Join the chanting so as not to look soft | Film it to show everyone later | Walk away and say nothing to anyone
test: «Jake pushed me yesterday so I'm gonna shove him back harder, fair's fair.» What does Jesus' teaching say? | Do not take revenge; break the cycle | Pay him back exactly the same | Wait and get him when no one is looking | Get your friends to shove him for you
test: A Muslim classmate asks, «Is your Golden Rule the same as ours?» What is a good answer? | Many faiths teach something very like it, including Islam | No, only Christians have ever taught it | Yes, but Christians made it up first and the others copied it | It is a rule about money, not about people
test: Your sister keeps telling everyone how messy her friend is, but her own room is a tip. Which teaching fits? | Take the plank out of your own eye first | Blessed are those who mourn | Store up treasure in heaven, not on earth | You are the light of the world
test: «Seriously, if someone's beating you up you just let them? that's mad.» What is a fair reply? | Loving enemies doesn't mean letting them hurt you; get help | Yes, Christians must let anyone hurt them | No, Christians are told to hit back first | It's mad, so Jesus never said anything about it
test: Your cousin is up all night worrying about the new school. Which part of the sermon speaks to that? | Do not worry; God cares for the birds and lilies | Love your enemies and pray for them | Salt that loses its taste is thrown out and trampled | Take the plank out of your own eye
test: A boy at Sunday school says, «Being salt and light means showing off how good you are.» What is right? | It means doing good so others give glory to God | Yes, the aim is to be praised by people | It means staying away from everyone else | It means only being good in church
test: «New phone, new trainers, new console — he's got everything, he must be happy.» Which saying makes you think again? | Where your treasure is, there your heart will be also | Blessed are the peacemakers | Do to others what you would have them do to you | Turn the other cheek
test: A judge asks: «Is turning the other cheek weak or strong?» Which answer is best reasoned? | Strong, because it takes courage to stop revenge, though Christians disagree how far it goes | Weak, because anyone who doesn't hit back is a coward | Strong, because the Bible says so and that settles it | Weak, because it never changes anything ever
test: A new girl gets left out of the game at break. Using the Golden Rule, what should you do? | Ask her to join, as you would want if you were new | Leave her alone, because she isn't really your friend yet | Wait to see if the others want her first | Tell her the game is full so she isn't hurt
exam: Identify the location within the Scriptures of the discourse designated the Sermon on the Mount. | The Gospel according to Matthew, chapters five to seven | The Book of Genesis, chapters one to three, at the very beginning | The Acts of the Apostles, chapter two | The Book of Psalms, Psalm twenty-three
exam: Pursuant to the Beatitudes, ascertain the designation conferred upon those who make peace. | Children of God | Rulers of the earth | Servants of the law | Masters of the Temple
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established formulation of the precept hereinafter referred to as the Golden Rule. | Do to others what you would have them do to you | Do to others what they have done to you | Do to others whatever is permitted by the law of the land | Do to others only what benefits yourself
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the teaching concerning the plank and the speck? | One ought to examine one's own faults before criticising those of others | One ought to point out the faults of others at once | Minor faults are graver than major ones | Carpentry presents a hazard to the eyes
